    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0d52119b-2896-54d1-973a-4e276ee066eb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-5+5x)^{2}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{2},\\,g=-5+5x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=2g\\cdot (5) \\\\  &=10(-5+5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c1e0c4cf-0a95-5708-9b37-d1d859ea57b4",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
